WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:04.279
Robert Downey Jr.'s Iron Man went through
a profound journey in Avengers: Endgame and

00:00:04.279 --> 00:00:07.960
delivered an unforgettable line that was a
touching callback to the very beginning of

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:09.300
the Marvel movie franchise.

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:13.960
As it turns out, that iconic moment almost
never happened… because Downey Jr. reportedly

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:16.300
didn't want to say Tony Stark's last major
line.

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:21.340
Even though co-directors Joe and Anthony Russo
lifted the ban on spoilers for Avengers: Endgame,

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:24.120
we're offering you one last warning: Major
spoilers ahead!

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:26.440
And now for the juicy details.

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:30.919
During a recent appearance on CinemaBlend's
ReelBlend podcast, the Russo brothers opened

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:33.320
up about Tony's final scene in Avengers: Endgame.

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:37.160
As you might recall, the hero equipped his
own suit with the Infinity Stones, snapped

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:40.910
his fingers, and promptly turned Thanos and
his minions into space dust.

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:45.620
Right before Tony mirrored Thanos' Decimation
from Infinity War, the Mad Titan was clearly

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:48.080
convinced that he had the upper hand, proudly
proclaiming:

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:49.340
"I am inevitable."

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:53.720
But in a move displaying all the dry, cool,
wit that makes Tony who he is - he flashed

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:57.280
his hand to show Thanos he had the Infinity
Stones - and then he said it:

00:00:57.350 --> 00:00:58.340
"I am Iron Man."

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:01.559
Of course, the line is a callback to the end
of 2008's Iron Man.

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:02.840
"I am Iron Man."

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:11.100
Sadly, the Snap takes a toll on Tony's body,
and he dies shortly after that.

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:14.659
He manages to speak a few more words before
passing away… and then we get to hear him

00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:18.060
one more time in a heartwarming message that
he recorded prior to his death.

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:21.240
But Tony's last big line is this proud assertion
of his heroism.

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:22.520
But hold everything.

00:01:22.690 --> 00:01:26.700
As Joe and Anthony Russo tell it, Tony originally
didn't say those words at all.

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:29.700
In fact, he originally didn't say anything
in that moment.

00:01:29.909 --> 00:01:33.640
An early cut of the film simply had Tony snap
his fingers, and that was that.

00:01:33.649 --> 00:01:37.509
Enter Avengers: Endgame editor Jeff Ford,
who pitched the idea of having Tony say:

00:01:37.509 --> 00:01:38.509
"I am Iron Man."

00:01:38.509 --> 00:01:42.329
Once they heard the concept, the Russo brothers
knew they had to reshoot the scene with that

00:01:42.329 --> 00:01:42.960
line included.

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:46.320
But apparently Downey Jr. was initially uneasy
with the idea.

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:49.660
It took some convincing before he finally
agreed to do a reshoot.

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:50.780
As Joe Russo revealed:

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:56.200
"I had dinner with him like two weeks before
we were supposed to shoot it.

00:01:56.820 --> 00:01:58.280
And he was like, 'I don't know.

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:00.680
I don't really want to go back and get into
that emotional state.'"

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:05.200
Joe Russo goes on to explain that producer
Joel Silver is a close personal pal of Downey

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:07.800
Jr.'s, and he just so happened to be eating
with them.

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:10.820
According to Joe, Silver enthusiastically
blurted out:

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:11.979
"'Robert, what are you talking about?

00:02:11.979 --> 00:02:14.190
That's the greatest line I've ever heard!

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:15.930
You gotta say this line!

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:17.040
You have to do this!'"

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:21.900
Once Downey Jr. was on board, the crew reportedly
headed back to the studio to reshoot the scene.

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:26.090
As we said before, the newly-added line served
as a touching nod to the very beginning of

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:26.920
the MCU.

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:31.000
But the whole reshooting process turned out
to be a callback to Downey Jr.'s humble beginnings

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:31.720
as Iron Man.

00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:33.280
As Anthony Russo explains:

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:40.720
"The stage that we shot that shot on was adjacent
to the stage where Robert Downey Jr. screen

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:42.890
tested for Iron Man."

00:02:42.890 --> 00:02:45.520
Well… if that doesn't give you chills, we
don't know what will.

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:49.480
Downey Jr. might have been wary about stepping
back into character for the powerful scene,

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:51.700
but we're so glad he ultimately came around.

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:55.690
The final line he shot is easily one of the
most memorable moments in the entire film.

00:02:55.690 --> 00:02:59.989
And, as many fans can attest, Avengers: Endgame
just wouldn't have been the same without Tony

00:02:59.989 --> 00:03:02.600
Stark going out with a bang… and a biting
remark.

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:04.680
That's sort of his calling card, you know?

